2n/(2(d−2))3n/6, and it\nseems not impossible that this is extremal:\nConjecture 5.2. For any d-regular, n-vertex G, mis(G) ≤2n/(2(d−2))3n/6.\nThis would be analogous to the fact—recently proved in spectacularly simple\nfashion by Yufei Zhao [14] (but by reducing to the bipartite case proved a\nlittle less simply in [9])—that the total number of independent sets in such\na G is at most (2d+1 −1)n/(2d), a value achieved by a disjoint union of Kdd’s\nwhenever 2d|n. One would, of course, hope to also have analogues for the\nother parts of Theorem 1.2; but we don’t see good candidates for these, and\nsuspect that they do not have clean answers.\n10--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Tractable Variant of Cover Time (https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.6605)\nciteturn0academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.4 years ago; Title: A Tractable Variant of Cover Time ... The two values are not very far from each other, and so cover cost might be a useful tool in the study of cover time.\nTitle: A Tractable Variant of Cover Time\nAuthors: Agelos Georgakopoulos\nDate: Thu Jun 28 09:40:06 2012\n\nWe introduce a variant of the cover time of a graph, called cover cost, in which the cost of a step is proportional to the number of yet uncovered vertices. It turns out that cover cost is more tractable than cover time; we provide an $O(n^4)$ algorithm for its computation, as well as some explicit formulae. The two values are not very far from each other, and so cover cost might be a useful tool in the study of cover time.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Tractable Variant of Cover Time (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=a1897a9b3ee8a404cfcdf9d3d864afb22c967874&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; A Tractable Variant of Cover Time ... cost might be a useful tool in the study of cover time.\nA Tractable Variant of Cover Time\nAgelos Georgakopoulos∗\nMay 2, 2014\nAbstract\nWe introduce a variant of the cover time of a graph, called cover cost,\nin which the cost of a step is proportional to the number of yet uncovered\nvertices. It turns out that cover cost is more tractable than cover time; we\nprovide an O(n4) algorithm for its computation, as well as some explicit\nformulae. The two values are not very far from each other, and so cover\ncost might be a useful tool in the study of cover time.\n1\nIntroduction\nThe cover time CTr(G) of a graph G from a vertex r is the expected num-\nber of steps it takes for random walk on G starting at r to visit all vertices.\nThe cover time of G is deﬁned as CT (G) := maxr∈V (G) CTr(G). It has been\nextensively studied in various contexts; applications include the construction\nof universal traversal sequences [AKL+79, Bro90], testing graph connectivity\n[AKL+79, KR95], and protocol testing [MP94]. It has been studied by physi-\ncists interested in the fractal structure of the uncovered set of a ﬁnite grid; see\n[DPRZ01] for references and for an interesting relation between the cover time\nof a ﬁnite grid and Brownian motion on Riemannian manifolds.\nMany bounds on cover time for speciﬁc classes of graphs have been obtained,\nsee e.g. [BW90, Fei95, GWb] and references therein. However, in general it is\nvery hard to obtain exact formulae for cover time, or compute it algorithmically.\nA question of [AF] that remained open for many years despite several eﬀorts\n[Mat88, KKLV00], and was recently resolved in the aﬃrmative using heavy\nprobabilistic machinery [DLP], is whether there is a deterministic algorithm\nwhich approximates CT (G) up to a constant factor in polynomial time.\narXiv:1206.6605v1  [math.CO]  28 Jun 2012\nThe following situation suggests a similar concept, which we will call the\ncover cost of G. Suppose that a truck starts at r loaded with some goods to\nbe equally distributed to the other vertices of the graph and performs random\nwalk, leaving the goods corresponding to each vertex the ﬁrst time it gets there\nand carrying all remaining goods along. Rather than the expected number of\nsteps it will take to ﬁnish this tour (which equals CTr(G)), the truck driver\nis more interested in the expected total amount of goods that will have to be\ncarried.\n∗Supported by FWF Grant P-24028-N18.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCounting Maximal Antichains and Independent Sets (https://doi.org/10.1007/S11083-012-9253-5)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Ilinca^{1} & ... The results in (ii) are implied by more general upper bounds on the numbers of maximal independent sets in regular and biregular graphs. ... Zhao, Y.: The number of independent sets in a regular graph.\n\n# Counting Maximal Antichains and Independent Sets\n\n  * Published: 09 March 2012\n\n  * Volume 30, pages 427–435 (2013)\n\n  * L. Ilinca^{1} &\n  * J. Kahn^{2} \n\n  * 303 Accesses\n\n  * 14 Citations\n\n  * Explore all metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nAnswering several questions of Duffus, Frankl and Rödl, we give asymptotics for the logarithms of (i) the number of maximal antichains in the n-dimensional Boolean algebra and (ii) the numbers of maximal independent sets in the covering graph of the n-dimensional hypercube and certain natural subgraphs thereof. The results in (ii) are implied by more general upper bounds on the numbers of maximal independent sets in regular and biregular graphs. We also mention some stronger possibilities involving actual rather than logarithmic asymptotics.\n\nThis is a preview of subscription content, to check access.\n\n## References\n\n  1. Chung, F.R.K., Frankl, P., Graham, R., Shearer, J.B.: Some intersection theorems for ordered sets and graphs. Proc. Am. Math. Soc. 21, 677–682 (1969)\n\n  11. McEliece, R.J.: The Theory of Information and Coding. Addison-Wesley, London (1977)\n\n  12. Moon, J.W., Moser, L.: On cliques in graphs. Isr. J. Math. 3, 23–28 (1965)\n\n  13. Sapozhenko, A.A.: The number of independent sets in graphs. Moscow Univ. Math. Bull. 62, 116–118 (2007)\n\n  14. Zhao, Y.: The number of independent sets in a regular graph. Comb. Probab. Comput. 19, 315–320 (2010)\n\n### Authors and Affiliations\n\n  1. Department of Mathematics, Indiana University, Bloomington, IN, 47405, USA\n\nL. Ilinca\n\n  2. Department of Mathematics, Rutgers University, Piscataway, NJ, 08854, USA\n\nJ. Kahn\n\n### Corresponding author\n\nCorrespondence to J. Kahn.\n\n### Cite this article\n\nIlinca, L., Kahn, J. Counting Maximal Antichains and Independent Sets. Order 30, 427–435 (2013). https://doi.org/10.1007/s11083-012-9253-5\n\n  * Received: 17 August 2011\n\n  * Accepted: 22 February 2012\n\n  * Published: 09 March 2012\n\n  * Issue date: July 2013\n\n  * DOI (Digital Object Identifier): https://doi.org/10.1007/s11083-012-9253-5\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Tractable Variant of Cover Time (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/228083399_A_Tractable_Variant_of_Cover_Time)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # A Tractable Variant of Cover Time ... We introduce a variant of the cover time of a graph, called cover cost, in which the cost of a step is proportional to the number of yet uncovered vertices. ... The two values are not very far from each other, and so cover cost might be a useful tool in the study of cover time. ... Discover the world's research\n\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# A Tractable Variant of Cover Time\n\n  * June 2012\n\n  * Source\n  * arXiv\n\nAuthors:\n\nAgelos Georgakopoulos\n\n  * University of Warwick\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe introduce a variant of the cover time of a graph, called cover cost, in which the cost of a step is proportional to the number of yet uncovered vertices. It turns out that cover cost is more tracta